::GEO_U01_L04_GuidedPractice_Q01::
Question ID: GEO_U01_L04_GP_Q01
MLA Standard: MLA.GEO.FND.1
Question:
Which tool is used to transfer the length of a segment during a construction?
{
=A. Compass#Correct. A compass holds a fixed opening so a segment length can be transferred accurately.
~B. Protractor#A protractor measures angles, but it is not the tool used to transfer a segment length in this construction.
~C. Calculator#A calculator does not construct or transfer geometric distances.
~D. Graphing grid#A grid may help organize a drawing, but it does not transfer a segment length with construction precision.
}

::GEO_U01_L04_GuidedPractice_Q02::
Question ID: GEO_U01_L04_GP_Q02
MLA Standard: MLA.GEO.FND.1
Question:
After opening the compass to the length of segment AB, what should happen before marking the copy?
{
~A. Change the compass opening to make the copy fit.#Changing the opening changes the length, so the copy may not match segment AB.
=B. Keep the compass opening fixed.#Correct. Keeping the compass opening fixed preserves the original segment length.
~C. Use the protractor to measure AB.#A protractor measures angles, not segment length.
~D. Estimate the length by sight.#Estimation by sight is not a precise construction method.
}

::GEO_U01_L04_GuidedPractice_Q03::
Question ID: GEO_U01_L04_GP_Q03
MLA Standard: MLA.GEO.FND.1
Question:
When copying an angle, why are arcs drawn from the original vertex and the new vertex?
{
~A. They make the drawing look more complete.#Arcs are not decorative; they provide construction evidence.
~B. They measure the angle with a protractor.#The construction uses compass arcs, not protractor measurement.
=C. They help transfer the original angle opening.#Correct. Matching arcs and compass distances place the copied angle accurately.
~D. They label the angle with a new name.#Naming an angle does not copy its opening; the arcs provide the construction evidence.
}

::GEO_U01_L04_GuidedPractice_Q04::
Question ID: GEO_U01_L04_GP_Q04
MLA Standard: MLA.GEO.FND.1
Question:
Which statement shows a correct segment-copy result?
{
~A. CD is longer than AB#A copied segment should match the original length, not be longer.
=B. CD = AB#Correct. If segment CD is copied from segment AB, their lengths should be equal.
~C. CD has a different length than AB#A copied segment should match the original length, not have a different length.
~D. CD is an angle copy#CD names a segment, not an angle.
}

::GEO_U01_L04_GuidedPractice_Q05::
Question ID: GEO_U01_L04_GP_Q05
MLA Standard: MLA.GEO.FND.1
Question:
Which mistake would make an angle copy unreliable?
{
~A. Drawing an initial ray from the new vertex#An initial ray is needed as a starting side for the copied angle.
~B. Drawing an arc from the original angle vertex#This is part of the angle-copy construction.
~C. Using the same compass opening on the new angle#Using the same opening helps transfer the original angle opening.
=D. Placing the second ray by sight instead of using arc intersections#Correct. An angle copy should use arcs and compass distances, not estimation by appearance.
}

